Crucible. The  co-opʼs  local  AI  server  in  the  story:  a  rack  of  salvaged
hardware where models are tested before anyone relies on them. More
generally, any local machine where you run and check models yourself.
(Chapter 11)
CSA (community-supported agriculture). An arrangement where house-
holds share in a farmʼs harvest, and its risk, directly. (Chapter 12)
Cyberdeck. A personal computer assembled or adapted around functions
you want to control, often using documented parts and offline software.
The particular hardware and software determine how repairable and self-
contained it is. (Chapter 5 and Appendix E)
DC-native. Running suitable loads on direct current, avoiding an unnec-
essary conversion to household AC and back. Charge controllers, voltage
converters, batteries, and wiring still have losses. (Chapter 17)
Deglobalization. Shortening the supply lines we depend on for survival,
so a shock on the other side of the planet doesnʼt reach all the way to your
dinner. Not isolation, and not the same as “decentralization.” (Chapter 9)
The Entitled and the Ready. Two mindsets toward the change, not two
kinds of people: needing the hierarchy to hold, or needing the suffering
to stop. Most of us carry a little of both. (Chapter 7)
Event Horizon. The view that once something smarter than us exists, the
far side becomes unpredictable to everyone, builders included. This book
uses it for the map. (Chapter 1)
Food baseline. Adequate food, available to everyone regardless of income
or employment, as a dependable service rather than a favor. The first
commitment of the Adequate Level of Care. (Introduction and Appendix
A)
Food first. The bookʼs priority order: food, then land care, cleanup, shel-
ter, and everything further out. A place to start, not a waiting list. (Intro-
duction)
Ground lease. A long lease on land, often ninety-nine years in a land
trust, that lets people own their home or greenhouse without owning the
dirt under it. (Chapter 15)
